(window.HALCYON_MOCK=window.HALCYON_MOCK||[]).push({ part:`Pr2 Process`, form:2, formName:`Practice exam 2`,
// case studies for form 2 (items in any form 2 part point at one with case:`id`, cq:n is the item's place in it; see mock/README.md)
cases:[
  { id:`m2c1`, title:`Case: the water utility's billing app`, text:[
    `A city water utility is replacing paper bills with a customer app and web portal. An outside firm supplies five developers under a time and materials contract, and they work in two week sprints beside two of the utility's own developers. The product owner is the customer service manager, who also runs the call center.`,
    `A new state rule takes effect in 14 weeks. From that date, every bill, paper or digital, must show the customer's water use next to the same month last year. About 60 points of the backlog relate to the rule. The whole backlog is 210 points, and velocity over the last five sprints was 24, 27, 25, 26, and 23.`,
    `The firm's invoices for the last two sprints billed about 10 percent more hours than the agreed team plan, and two stories the firm reported as done failed the utility's acceptance tests.`,
    `The state agency that wrote the rule has offered to review draft bill layouts before the start date. Two weeks later, nobody on the project has answered.`,
    `In a code review last week, a utility developer and a firm developer argued. Since then, each has been merging code without asking the other for a review.`] },
  { id:`m2c2`, title:`Case: the bottling line move`, text:[
    `A beverage company is moving a bottling line from an old plant to a new building 40 miles away, under a predictive plan. The budget at completion is $4.0 million over 10 months. A rigging firm moves the equipment under a fixed price contract, and the company's own engineers will recommission it.`,
    `At the end of month 4, planned value is $1.6 million, earned value is $1.2 million, and actual cost is $1.5 million.`,
    `About 200 staff work on the line. Their union has asked how shift patterns will change at the new building, and the company hasn't answered yet. Several senior operators have said they'll retire rather than make the commute.`,
    `The risk register shows a 30 percent chance that the rigging firm's heavy crane won't be available in month 7. If it isn't, the move slips two weeks at a cost of $90,000. Booking a second crane on standby would cost $20,000 and remove the risk.`,
    `The old plant's wastewater treatment unit can't be moved, and the plan says it will be scrapped. The company leases the old site, and nobody on the project has read what the lease requires when the company leaves.`] }
],
questions:[

{ q:`A client's contract requires monthly reports on how much construction waste a new hospital project keeps out of landfill. The project management plan doesn't measure waste at all. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Build the waste measure and its reporting into the plan`,
        `Ask the site contractor to estimate waste at the end`,
        `Report waste figures only if the client asks for them`,
        `Add a line about waste to the next status report`],
  a:0, why:`Sustainability reporting that the contract requires is a critical information requirement, so the plan needs to say what's measured, by whom, how and when, before the waste starts leaving the site. A line in the next status report is the near miss. It acknowledges the need, but without a defined measure and owner there's nothing reliable to report. An estimate at the end and reporting on request both miss the contract's monthly requirement.`,
  task:`R1`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`An agile team's release plan puts a major feature in sprint 9. The predictive hardware plan books the only integration lab for weeks 12 to 14, which fall in sprints 6 and 7. The two plans were built separately. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Ask the agile team to finish the feature by sprint 6`,
        `Bring both plan owners together to align the dates`,
        `Book a second integration lab for the later sprints`,
        `Let the integration test go ahead without the feature`],
  a:1, why:`An integrated plan means its parts agree. The project manager gets both plan owners together to find a fix that holds for both, perhaps moving the lab booking, splitting the feature or reordering the backlog. A second lab is the near miss. It might be one outcome, but it adds cost before anyone has looked at cheaper fixes. Forcing the agile team's date, or testing without the feature, solves one plan's problem by breaking the other.`,
  task:`R1`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`A new team member asks which of these is part of the project management plan rather than a project document. Which should the project manager name?`,
  opts:[`The issue log`,
        `The scope baseline`,
        `The risk register`,
        `The lessons learned register`],
  a:1, why:`The project management plan holds the baselines for scope, schedule and cost, plus the subsidiary management plans. The scope baseline, made up of the scope statement, the WBS and the WBS dictionary, is part of it. The risk register is the near miss. It's closely tied to the risk management plan, but it's a project document that changes often, not part of the plan itself. The issue log and the lessons learned register are project documents too.`,
  task:`R1`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`An AI assistant drafts a full project management plan from the organization's template in a few minutes. The sponsor wants to approve it the same day. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Review the draft with the plan owners before approval`,
        `Send the draft for approval and correct it during execution`,
        `Write the plan again by hand without using the AI draft`,
        `Ask the AI assistant to check its own draft for errors`],
  a:0, why:`AI output is a starting point, and someone accountable has to check that it fits this project: its scope, constraints, risks and people. Reviewing it with the plan owners does that before it becomes the baseline. Rewriting by hand is the near miss. It's careful, but it throws away a useful draft instead of checking it. Approving now and fixing later bakes errors into the baseline, and asking the tool to check itself isn't human oversight.`,
  task:`R1`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`A change to a pump's design has been approved. The project manager updates the scope baseline but nothing else. What has the project manager missed?`,
  opts:[`Getting a new sponsor signature on the scope baseline`,
        `Logging the change in the lessons learned register`,
        `Telling the team that the scope has changed`,
        `Updating every plan part the change affects`],
  a:3, why:`An approved change can ripple into the schedule, the cost baseline, risks, quality and procurement. Integrated change control means updating every affected part of the plan and the project documents, then telling the people affected. Telling the team is the near miss. It's needed, but the team would then work from a plan whose schedule and budget don't match the new scope. A fresh sponsor signature isn't usually needed for an approved change, and lessons learned come later.`,
  task:`R1`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`On a hybrid project, a hardware subcontractor and two agile software teams each submit their own plan. When the plans are merged, every one of them assumes someone else is arranging access to the customer's site. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Ask the customer to arrange access for everyone`,
        `Give site access an owner in the integrated plan`,
        `Let each plan owner arrange its own site access`,
        `Note the gap in the risk register for next month`],
  a:1, why:`Consolidating plans is how gaps like this come to light, and the fix is to put the missing work into the integrated plan with an owner and dates, linked to the work that needs it. Asking the customer is the near miss. The customer may grant access, but someone on the project still has to plan and request it. Separate arrangements risk three conflicting requests, and a risk entry records the gap without closing it.`,
  task:`R1`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`Two agile teams' release plans both assume the shared platform team will build their features in sprint 5. The platform team has capacity for only one of them. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Let the platform team pick which request it takes`,
        `Plan it jointly with all three teams`,
        `Ask both teams to plan their features for sprint 6`,
        `Escalate the conflict to the steering committee now`],
  a:1, why:`Dependencies between teams are best worked out together, with the teams and their product owners agreeing on order, value and options in one room. Letting the platform team pick is the near miss. They know their capacity, but they don't own the business priority between the two features. Moving both to sprint 6 just moves the clash, and escalating skips a step the teams can likely handle.`,
  task:`R1`, domain:`Process`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`In the utility app case, how should the team make sure the bills will meet the new rule?`,
  opts:[`Have the utility's lawyers review the app after release`,
        `Write the rule into the acceptance criteria of its stories`,
        `Add a single story to the backlog called "meet the rule"`,
        `Test the rule's requirements in the last sprint before the date`],
  a:1, why:`Turning the rule into acceptance criteria on the stories it touches makes it part of what done means, and it traces each requirement to work the team can test every sprint. Testing in the last sprint is the near miss. It checks the rule, but too late to fix problems before the date. A single vague story can't be estimated or tested, and a legal review after release is too late.`,
  task:`R2`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid`, case:`m2c1`, cq:2 },

{ q:`Two teams read the same work package name, "site preparation," differently. One thinks it includes removing old foundations, and the other doesn't. Which document should settle it?`,
  opts:[`The project charter`,
        `The requirements documentation`,
        `The activity list`,
        `The WBS dictionary`],
  a:3, why:`The WBS dictionary describes each work package in detail, including what's in it and what isn't, so it's where "site preparation" gets defined. If it's silent, the project manager updates it. The requirements documentation is the near miss. It says what the product must do, but not how the work is divided into packages. The charter is too high level, and the activity list breaks packages into activities without settling the boundary.`,
  task:`R2`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`During backlog refinement, stakeholders label nearly every requirement a "must have." The release can't hold them all. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Help the product owner and stakeholders rank items by value`,
        `Ask the team to estimate every item before ranking`,
        `Accept all the must haves and extend the release date`,
        `Let the team choose the items that are easiest to build`],
  a:0, why:`When everything is a must have, the label stops meaning anything. The project manager helps the product owner and stakeholders weigh items by value, often with a rule that only part of a release can be must haves. Estimating first is the near miss. Size matters to ordering, but estimates won't settle a disagreement about value. Extending the date avoids the choices, and building the easiest items ignores value entirely.`,
  task:`R2`, domain:`Process`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`A project needs input from 3,000 field technicians spread across the country about the tools they use. Which technique gathers that input most efficiently?`,
  opts:[`One on one interviews with each technician`,
        `A focus group held at headquarters`,
        `Observation of the technicians at one site`,
        `A questionnaire sent to all of the technicians`],
  a:3, why:`Questionnaires reach a large, spread out group quickly, and the answers can be summarized statistically. Interviews and focus groups can then follow up on what the survey finds. A focus group is the near miss. It gives rich discussion, but only a few people take part, and holding it at headquarters leaves out most of the field. Interviewing 3,000 people one at a time is impractical, and watching one site may miss differences elsewhere.`,
  task:`R2`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`A product backlog has grown to 600 items. Many are two years old, and nobody remembers why some were added. What should happen?`,
  opts:[`The product owner prunes and refines it with the team`,
        `The team estimates all 600 items in story points`,
        `The project manager archives every item over a year old`,
        `The stakeholders vote on which of the items to keep`],
  a:0, why:`A backlog is a living, ordered list owned by the product owner, so stale items come out and the rest get refined with the team. Removing what nobody needs keeps the backlog useful. Archiving by age is the near miss. It shrinks the list fast, but some old items may still carry value, and the decision belongs to the product owner, not the project manager. Estimating all 600 wastes effort on items that may go, and a vote isn't how a backlog gets ordered.`,
  task:`R2`, domain:`Process`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`Workers on a packing line describe their process in interviews, but the steps they list don't match the line's error reports. The project is collecting requirements for a new packing system. Which technique should the project manager add?`,
  opts:[`Observing the workers on the line as they do the job`,
        `A questionnaire sent to every worker on the line`,
        `A second round of interviews with the line supervisors`,
        `A review of the old system's user manual`],
  a:0, why:`People often leave out steps they do by habit, so watching the work as it happens shows what they actually do, including the workarounds behind the errors. Observation fills the gaps interviews leave. More interviews with supervisors are the near miss. Supervisors know the process as designed, but they don't do the job every day, so they're likely to miss the same habits. A questionnaire asks the same people the same kind of question, and an old manual shows how the line was meant to run, not how it runs.`,
  task:`R2`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`On a hybrid project, the users of a new dispatch screen can't describe what they need, but they say they'll know it when they see it. What should the project manager suggest?`,
  opts:[`Build a quick prototype and gather their feedback`,
        `Write detailed requirements from the old screen`,
        `Ask the users to fill in a requirements template`,
        `Let the developers design the screen on their own`],
  a:0, why:`When users can't describe what they want, a prototype gives them something to react to, and their feedback turns into requirements. It's an elicitation technique made for this case. Copying the old screen is the near miss. It's a reasonable starting point, but it carries over the old screen's problems and ignores what users need now. A template asks for exactly what they can't give, and leaving it to the developers removes the users.`,
  task:`R2`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`A team building a work breakdown structure leaves out project management work, saying it isn't part of the product. What should the project manager tell them?`,
  opts:[`The WBS covers all of the work, including managing it`,
        `Project management work belongs only in the schedule`,
        `Leaving it out is fine on a small project like this`,
        `Project management work goes in the risk register instead`],
  a:0, why:`The 100 percent rule says the WBS includes all the work of the project, product and project work alike, and nothing outside it. Project management takes effort, cost and time, so it belongs in the WBS. Saying it can be left out on a small project is the near miss. Small projects need less management work, not none. Putting it only in the schedule or the risk register leaves it unplanned and unbudgeted.`,
  task:`R2`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`An agile team isn't sure whether a new checkout design will raise sales. It could release the design to every user at once, or to a share of users first. What should the team do?`,
  opts:[`Release it to every user and watch total sales`,
        `Release it to some users and compare with the rest`,
        `Ask a focus group which design they prefer`,
        `Hold it until the full redesign is finished`],
  a:1, why:`Releasing a change to part of the users and comparing their results with everyone else's shows whether the change itself made the difference. It's a delivery option built to demonstrate value. Releasing to everyone is the near miss. It delivers to all sooner, but sales move for many reasons, so a before and after total can't show what the design did. A focus group reports preference, not behavior, and holding the release delays both value and learning.`,
  task:`R3`, domain:`Process`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`An agile team finishes features every two weeks, but operations releases software only once a quarter. Finished work waits up to three months before customers can use it. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Ask the team to slow down to match the quarterly window`,
        `Bundle more features into each quarterly release`,
        `Work with operations on more frequent releases`,
        `Let the team release directly without operations`],
  a:2, why:`Value isn't delivered until customers can use it. Working with operations on a safe, more frequent release path gets finished increments to customers sooner. Bundling more features is the near miss. It makes each release look bigger, but it adds risk and leaves value waiting just as long. Slowing the team wastes capacity, and releasing around operations ignores the controls they're there to keep.`,
  task:`R3`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`Two features are about the same size. One supports a holiday promotion and loses most of its value if it isn't live by late November. The other has no deadline but slightly higher value overall. Which should the product owner schedule first?`,
  opts:[`The feature with no deadline and the higher overall value`,
        `The holiday feature, whose value drops if it's late`,
        `Whichever one the team would rather build first`,
        `Both at once, with the team split in half`],
  a:1, why:`Cost of delay counts how value changes with time. The holiday feature's value falls off a cliff after its date, while the other loses little by waiting, so the holiday feature goes first. The higher value feature is the near miss. Total value matters, but ignoring timing can lose more value overall. Team preference isn't a measure of value, and splitting the team slows both.`,
  task:`R3`, domain:`Process`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`A project will cost $240,000 and is expected to save $8,000 a month once it's running. What is its payback period?`,
  opts:[`24 months`,
        `20 months`,
        `30 months`,
        `36 months`],
  a:2, why:`Payback period is the initial cost divided by the savings per period: 240,000 divided by 8,000 is 30 months. 24 months is the near miss. It's what dividing by 10,000 a month gives, not 8,000. 20 and 36 months come from other wrong rates. Payback is simple, but it ignores the time value of money and anything after the payback point, so it's best used beside measures like net present value.`,
  task:`R3`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`A hybrid project will replace a hospital's paper forms. Clinicians, finance and compliance each see its value differently: time saved, lower cost and fewer audit findings. What should the project manager do early on?`,
  opts:[`Agree with them which parts of value matter and how to show each`,
        `Track the cost savings, since they're the easiest to measure`,
        `Let the sponsor choose one measure of value for everyone`,
        `Leave value questions until the first release is live`],
  a:0, why:`Value usually has several parts, and each stakeholder group sees a different one. Agreeing which parts matter, and how each will be shown, gives the project targets that everyone recognizes. Letting the sponsor choose is the near miss. The sponsor has the final say on the business case, but one measure picked alone can leave clinicians and compliance unconvinced. Tracking only the easiest measure ignores the rest of the value, and waiting for the release leaves the team building without targets.`,
  task:`R3`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`At a phase gate review, updated market data shows the project's expected benefits have fallen below its remaining costs. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Continue the project as planned to protect the investment`,
        `Cut scope quietly until the costs match the benefits`,
        `Present the updated business case for a decision`,
        `Wait for the next gate to see whether the market recovers`],
  a:2, why:`Phase gates exist to check whether the project is still worth doing. The project manager presents the updated business case honestly, and the sponsor or governance body decides whether to continue, change or stop. Waiting for the next gate is the near miss. Markets do change, but waiting keeps spending on a case that no longer holds. Continuing to protect money already spent is the sunk cost trap, and quiet scope cuts hide the problem from the people who must decide.`,
  task:`R3`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`A hybrid project delivers a new warehouse conveyor, a fixed 12 month hardware build, and its control software, built in sprints. Staff could use parts of the software, such as the inventory screens, on the old conveyor right away. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Hold the software until the new conveyor is installed`,
        `Release the usable parts to staff as they're ready`,
        `Build the software for the new conveyor only`,
        `Release the software once all of it is finished`],
  a:1, why:`Incremental delivery gets value to people as soon as it's usable. Releasing the inventory screens now gives staff the benefit, and the team gets feedback months earlier. Releasing once everything is finished is the near miss. It avoids managing two versions, but it gives up months of value and learning. Holding the software for the conveyor, or building it for the new conveyor only, wastes the same chance.`,
  task:`R3`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`While building the schedule, the project manager assigns a welding specialist to a critical activity in August. The specialist has three weeks of leave booked that month. What should the project manager have checked?`,
  opts:[`The resource breakdown structure`,
        `The responsibility assignment matrix`,
        `The resource calendar`,
        `The team charter`],
  a:2, why:`Resource calendars show when each person or resource is available, including leave and holidays, and they're checked whenever activities are scheduled. The responsibility assignment matrix is the near miss. It shows who does what, but not when they're available. The resource breakdown structure groups resources by type, and the team charter sets norms.`,
  task:`R4`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`Steel for a building arrives six weeks before it's needed, and the site has no secure place to store it. Some of it has already been damaged by weather. What should the project manager do for future deliveries?`,
  opts:[`Rent extra storage for future steel deliveries`,
        `Time deliveries to the schedule with the supplier`,
        `Ask the supplier to cover the cost of the damage`,
        `Order extra steel to allow for weather damage`],
  a:1, why:`Physical resources should arrive when the work needs them. Coordinating delivery dates with the supplier against the schedule, often called just in time, cuts storage cost and damage. Renting storage is the near miss. It protects the steel, but it pays to fix a timing problem that can be fixed for free. Asking the supplier to pay for weather damage on the buyer's site is unlikely to work, and ordering extra wastes money.`,
  task:`R4`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`An agile team has worked together for a year and delivers at a steady pace. To spread skills, a department manager plans to swap two of its members with another team's every quarter. What should the project manager recommend?`,
  opts:[`Keep the team stable and spread skills in other ways`,
        `Swap two members every quarter as the manager plans`,
        `Swap only the newest members, who know the least`,
        `Replace the swaps with contractors who move between teams`],
  a:0, why:`Stable teams keep the trust and working habits that make their pace predictable, and each reshuffle sends a team back through forming and storming. Keeping the team together, and spreading skills through pairing across teams or a community of practice, meets the manager's goal without that cost. Swapping only the newest members is the near miss. It disturbs less, but it still breaks the team up every quarter and moves the people who are still learning. The full swap resets both teams four times a year, and rotating contractors makes every team temporary.`,
  task:`R4`, domain:`Process`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`The project asked for a senior database engineer. The functional manager offers a junior engineer instead, saying no senior engineers are free for six months. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Turn down the junior engineer and wait for a senior one to free up`,
        `Accept the junior engineer with no further changes`,
        `Assess the gap and agree on support for the junior engineer`,
        `Escalate to the sponsor to force a senior assignment`],
  a:2, why:`Acquiring resources often means negotiating. The project manager looks at what the junior engineer can and can't do, agrees with the functional manager on support such as training, mentoring or senior review, and updates the risk register. Escalating to the sponsor is the near miss. It might be needed if the gap is critical, but forcing an assignment that doesn't exist strains the relationship. Refusing delays the work for months, and accepting without a plan ignores the risk.`,
  task:`R4`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`A crew lays 40 meters of pipe per working day. A 1,200 meter section must be finished in 10 working days. How many crews are needed?`,
  opts:[`4 crews`,
        `3 crews`,
        `2 crews`,
        `30 crews`],
  a:1, why:`One crew lays 40 meters a day, or 400 meters in 10 days, so 1,200 meters takes 3 crews. 4 crews is the near miss. It would finish early, in seven and a half days, but at the cost of a crew the work doesn't need. 2 crews would take 15 days, and 30 is the total number of crew days, not the number of crews.`,
  task:`R4`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`A developer on an agile team is split across three projects. Their stories often sit half done, and the team's cycle time is rising. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Ask the developer to track time on each project`,
        `Work with the managers to cut the developer's assignments`,
        `Give the developer only small stories from now on`,
        `Ask the team to finish the developer's stories for them`],
  a:1, why:`Splitting a person across projects costs time every time they switch, and it leaves work waiting. The fix is to reduce the split, working with the managers who set the assignments. Small stories are the near miss. They help work flow, but they don't remove the switching that's causing the delay. Tracking time measures the problem without fixing it, and having others finish the stories hides it.`,
  task:`R4`, domain:`Process`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`A vendor's specialist and the internal test team both need the project's only test rig in the same week. The vendor's work is on the critical path, and the internal tests have two weeks of float. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Give the vendor the rig and move the internal tests`,
        `Split the rig's week evenly between the two groups that need it`,
        `Let whoever booked the rig first keep the week`,
        `Rent a second test rig for that one week`],
  a:0, why:`When two activities compete for one resource, the critical path and float guide the call. The vendor's work can't slip without moving the end date, while the internal tests have room to move. Splitting the week is the near miss. It feels fair, but it delays the critical activity for no gain. First come, first served ignores the schedule, and a second rig costs money that the float already saves.`,
  task:`R4`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`An agile team decides it needs a user experience designer for two sprints to rework its onboarding screens. No one on the team has those skills. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Ask a developer to learn the design work in those sprints`,
        `Tell the team to postpone the onboarding work`,
        `Help the team bring in a designer from the design group`,
        `Have the product owner design the screens`],
  a:2, why:`A servant leader helps the team get what it needs, including skills it doesn't have. Working with the functional manager or resource owner to bring in a designer supports the team's decision. Having a developer learn it is the near miss. Building skills across the team is good, but learning design from scratch in two sprints risks poor screens. Postponing delays value, and the product owner owns what to build, not the design work.`,
  task:`R4`, domain:`Process`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`In the utility app case, what should the project manager do about the firm's invoices and the failed stories?`,
  opts:[`Withhold all payment until the hours are explained`,
        `Approve the invoices and track hours more closely`,
        `Ask the firm to replace the developers on the team`,
        `Verify the hours and raise both issues with the firm`],
  a:3, why:`Under time and materials the buyer pays for verified hours, and the vendor's performance is judged by accepted work, not hours billed. The project manager verifies the hours and raises both issues with the firm under the contract's terms. Withholding all payment is the near miss. Holding back disputed hours may be allowed, but holding everything before checking anything breaks the contract's terms and the relationship. Approving skips the check, and asking for new developers jumps past a conversation.`,
  task:`R5`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid`, case:`m2c1`, cq:3 },

{ q:`A buyer scores three proposals out of 10 on technical approach (weighted 50 percent), price (30 percent) and experience (20 percent). Vendor A scores 9, 5 and 8. Vendor B scores 7, 9 and 8. Vendor C scores 8, 7 and 6. Which vendor ranks highest?`,
  opts:[`Vendor A at 7.6`,
        `Vendor B at 7.8`,
        `Vendor C at 7.3`,
        `Vendor A on its technical score`],
  a:1, why:`Multiply each score by its weight and add. A is 4.5 plus 1.5 plus 1.6, or 7.6. B is 3.5 plus 2.7 plus 1.6, or 7.8. C is 4.0 plus 2.1 plus 1.2, or 7.3. Vendor A at 7.6 is the near miss. A has the best technical score, but B's price score puts it ahead once the weights apply. Choosing on the technical score alone ignores the criteria the buyer published.`,
  task:`R5`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`A buyer is signing a four-year contract for steel deliveries. Steel prices have swung widely, and the seller won't commit to one price for four years. Which contract type fits best?`,
  opts:[`Firm fixed price for the whole four year term`,
        `Fixed price with economic price adjustment`,
        `Cost plus award fee`,
        `Time and materials`],
  a:1, why:`A fixed price with economic price adjustment lets the price move with an agreed index, such as a published steel price, which protects both sides over a long term. Firm fixed price is the near miss. It gives the buyer certainty, but the seller won't take the price risk, or will price it in heavily. Cost plus award fee and time and materials suit work whose scope is uncertain, not a defined product with a volatile price.`,
  task:`R5`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`Before negotiating with the top-ranked bidder, the buyer's project manager learns that its price is 12 percent over budget, though its technical approach is the strongest. What should the project manager do to prepare?`,
  opts:[`Tell the bidder the budget figure at the start of talks`,
        `Award the work to the second bidder, which is in budget`,
        `Ask the bidder to cut its price by 12 percent to match`,
        `Set goals, limits and trade offs with the team before talks`],
  a:3, why:`A negotiation strategy is set before the talks: what the buyer must have, where it can give, and when it walks away, for example trading scope or payment terms for price. Asking for a flat 12 percent cut is the near miss. It names the gap, but a single demand with nothing to trade often stalls the talks or pushes the seller to cut quality quietly. Revealing the budget gives away the buyer's position, and going to the second bidder throws away the best technical offer before trying.`,
  task:`R5`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`The seller's project manager asks the buyer's project manager to approve a change to the delivery schedule by email. The contract says changes must be signed by the buyer's contracting officer. What should the buyer's project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Approve it by email to keep the work moving`,
        `Ask the seller to put the request in a letter`,
        `Show the new dates in the project schedule now`,
        `Send the request to the contracting officer`],
  a:3, why:`Only someone with contract authority can change a contract, and this contract names the contracting officer. The project manager routes the request there, through the contract's change process. Asking for a letter is the near miss. A formal request helps, but it still doesn't give the project manager authority to approve it. Approving by email or updating the schedule acts as if a change exists before it legally does.`,
  task:`R5`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`A seller has hired a subcontractor for part of the work. The buyer's project manager is unhappy with the subcontractor's progress and wants to tell its crew directly to change their sequence. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Give the subcontractor's crew the new sequence`,
        `Ask the subcontractor's owner to meet with the buyer`,
        `Raise the concern with the seller under the contract`,
        `Withhold payment from the subcontractor until it improves`],
  a:2, why:`The buyer's contract is with the seller, not the subcontractor, so directions go through the seller, who manages its own subcontractor. Asking the subcontractor's owner to meet is the near miss. It's a softer approach, but it still steps around the seller and blurs who's responsible. Directing the crew can lead to claims, and the buyer doesn't pay the subcontractor at all.`,
  task:`R5`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`A buyer is choosing between two shortlisted vendors to build a product with an agile approach. Both proposals look strong on paper. What's a good way to choose?`,
  opts:[`Pick the vendor that quotes the lower hourly rate`,
        `Ask each vendor for a detailed fixed scope plan`,
        `Pay each vendor for a short trial sprint`,
        `Choose the vendor with the most agile certifications`],
  a:2, why:`For agile work, seeing how a vendor actually works with the team tells the buyer more than any proposal. A short paid trial gives real evidence of delivery, collaboration and quality. A detailed plan is the near miss. It shows the vendor's thinking, but a fixed scope plan doesn't fit an agile approach, and it's still words on paper. The lowest rate ignores value, and certifications don't show how a vendor delivers.`,
  task:`R5`, domain:`Process`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`A company has committed to cutting its supply chain emissions. Its project is about to request proposals for electric delivery vans and a charging service. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Include sustainability criteria in how proposals are scored`,
        `Choose the lowest price and ask about emissions afterward`,
        `Mention the emissions goal in the request's cover letter only`,
        `Leave emissions to the fleet team after the vans arrive`],
  a:0, why:`Source selection criteria shape what sellers offer. Putting sustainability measures into the published evaluation criteria, with weights, means bids compete on them and the choice can be defended. Mentioning the goal in the cover letter is the near miss. It signals intent, but without scoring it doesn't affect which bid wins. Price first with questions later, and leaving it to the fleet team, both miss the point when it can still shape the contract.`,
  task:`R5`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`In the bottling line case, what cost performance would the remaining work need to finish within the budget, and what does that suggest?`,
  opts:[`About 0.89, close to the 0.80 achieved to date`,
        `About 0.80, the same as performance so far`,
        `About 1.25, which no recovery plan could reach`,
        `About 1.12, far above the 0.80 achieved to date`],
  a:3, why:`The to complete performance index is the work left divided by the money left: (4.0 minus 1.2) divided by (4.0 minus 1.5), or 2.8 divided by 2.5, which is 1.12. The CPI so far is 0.80 (1.2 divided by 1.5), so needing 1.12 from here puts the budget out of reach without a real change, and the project manager should raise a new forecast. 0.89 is the near miss. It's the same ratio turned upside down. 0.80 is the CPI itself, and 1.25 is 1 divided by the CPI.`,
  task:`R6`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive`, case:`m2c2`, cq:1 },

{ q:`Using the figure, what are the schedule and cost performance indexes at the status date?`,
  fig:`<prop type="chart" kind="evm" title="Earned value, $ thousands, months 1 to 10 (status date: end of month 6)" pv="40,90,150,220,290,360,420,470,500,520" ev="45,100,165,240,320,390" ac="50,115,190,275,365,445">At month 6: PV 360, EV 390, AC 445.</prop>`,
  opts:[`SPI about 0.92 and CPI about 1.14`,
        `SPI about 1.08 and CPI about 0.81`,
        `SPI about 0.81 and CPI about 1.08`,
        `SPI about 1.08 and CPI about 0.88`],
  a:3, why:`SPI is EV divided by PV: 390 divided by 360, or about 1.08, so the work is ahead of plan. CPI is EV divided by AC: 390 divided by 445, or about 0.88, so each dollar spent has earned about 88 cents of planned value. SPI 1.08 with CPI 0.81 is the near miss. The SPI is right, but 0.81 divides PV by AC, and cost performance compares the value earned with what it cost. The other two turn the ratios upside down or swap them.`,
  task:`R6`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`A project's budget at completion is $900,000. Its estimate at completion is now $1,035,000. What is the variance at completion, and what does it mean?`,
  opts:[`Plus $135,000, a forecast overrun`,
        `Plus $135,000, a forecast underrun`,
        `Minus $135,000, a forecast underrun`,
        `Minus $135,000, a forecast overrun`],
  a:3, why:`VAC is BAC minus EAC: $900,000 minus $1,035,000 is minus $135,000. A negative VAC means the project is forecast to finish over budget by that amount. Plus $135,000 as an overrun is the near miss. The meaning is right, but the formula subtracts the other way round, and the convention makes an overrun negative. The other two pair the number with an underrun, which would mean money left over.`,
  task:`R6`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`A project has a BAC of $800,000, PV of $400,000, EV of $300,000 and AC of $350,000. The original end date can't move, so both cost and schedule performance are expected to weigh on the remaining work. Which estimate at completion fits?`,
  opts:[`About $933,000`,
        `About $850,000`,
        `About $1.07 million`,
        `About $1.13 million`],
  a:3, why:`When both indexes are expected to drive the rest of the work, EAC is AC plus (BAC minus EV) divided by (CPI times SPI). CPI is 300 divided by 350, or about 0.857, and SPI is 300 divided by 400, or 0.75. Their product is about 0.643, and 500,000 divided by 0.643 is about 778,000, so EAC is about 350,000 plus 778,000, or $1.13 million. $933,000 is the near miss. It's BAC divided by CPI, which assumes only cost performance carries on. $850,000 assumes the rest goes to budget, and $1.07 million is BAC divided by SPI.`,
  task:`R6`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`A vendor's invoices reach the project about 60 days after the work is done. The project's earned value reports use paid invoices as actual cost. What's the problem, and what should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Earned value is too high, so hold it until invoices arrive`,
        `Actual cost is too low, so accrue for work not yet invoiced`,
        `Planned value is wrong, so rebaseline the schedule monthly`,
        `Nothing is wrong, and the reports can stay as they are`],
  a:1, why:`Actual cost should reflect the cost of work performed in the period, not when it's paid. With a 60 day lag, AC is too low, which makes CPI look better than it is. Accruing estimated costs for work done but not yet invoiced fixes that. Holding back earned value is the near miss. It lines EV and AC up in time, but it hides real progress and makes both measures late. Planned value is fine, and saying nothing is wrong lets the reports overstate cost performance.`,
  task:`R6`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`An agile product has a fixed budget for the year. Halfway through, the remaining funds will cover about eight more sprints, but the backlog would take twelve. What should happen?`,
  opts:[`The team speeds up to fit twelve sprints of work into eight`,
        `The product owner orders the backlog to fit the eight sprints`,
        `The project manager asks the sponsor for four more sprints of funding`,
        `The team cuts testing to finish more stories per sprint`],
  a:1, why:`With a fixed budget and flexible scope, the product owner orders the backlog by value so the most valuable work fits the funded sprints, and stakeholders see what won't make it. Asking for more funding is the near miss. It may be worth doing if the remaining items are valuable enough, but that's a business case for the product owner and sponsor, not a first step. Speeding up and cutting testing both ignore the team's real pace and quality.`,
  task:`R6`, domain:`Process`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`A hybrid project is 40 percent complete and has used 70 percent of its contingency reserve. Most of the biggest identified risks are still ahead. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Stop drawing on contingency until the project is half done`,
        `Reassess the remaining risks against what's left`,
        `Move management reserve into contingency to top it up`,
        `Keep going and report the reserve at closeout`],
  a:1, why:`A reserve burning faster than the work is a warning sign. The project manager reassesses the remaining risks against what's left, forecasts whether it will be enough, and reports the trend to the sponsor while there's still time to act. Moving management reserve is the near miss. It may end up being needed, but management reserve belongs to the sponsor or management and needs their approval, not a transfer by the project manager. Freezing contingency ignores risks that occur, and waiting until closeout is too late.`,
  task:`R6`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`A hybrid project pays a key supplier in euros. Since the budget was set, the exchange rate has moved 8 percent against the project. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Update the forecast and apply the plan's currency rules`,
        `Ask the supplier to absorb the change in the exchange rate`,
        `Wait for the rate to move back before reporting it`,
        `Cut scope in other areas to cover the difference`],
  a:0, why:`Currency movements are a known source of cost variance, and the financial management plan should say how they're handled, for example through contingency or a defined approval step. The project manager updates the forecast and follows those rules. Cutting scope elsewhere is the near miss. It might end up as one option, but deciding it alone skips the plan and the people who own scope. Pushing it onto the supplier ignores the contract, and waiting hides a real variance.`,
  task:`R6`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`The control chart shows fill weights for the last 20 samples. After sample 19, a supervisor wants to adjust the filling machine because it came close to the upper limit. What should the project manager advise?`,
  fig:`<prop type="chart" kind="control" title="Fill weight, grams, last 20 samples" mean="500" ucl="506" lcl="494" values="501,499,502,498,500,503,497,501,499,504,500,498,502,499,501,497,500,502,505,499"></prop>`,
  opts:[`Adjust the machine down. Sample 19 was near the limit.`,
        `Stop the line. The process is out of control.`,
        `Widen the limits. The samples vary too much.`,
        `Leave the machine alone. The process is in control.`],
  a:3, why:`Every point is inside the control limits, with no runs or trends, so the variation is common cause, the normal noise of the process. Adjusting a stable process in response to noise, sometimes called tampering, usually makes the variation worse. Adjusting down is the near miss. It feels proactive, but sample 19 is still inside the limits, and nothing points to an assignable cause. The process isn't out of control, and control limits come from the data, not from preference.`,
  task:`R7`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`Before welding starts, a project pays for its welders to take a certification course on the procedure they'll use. In cost of quality terms, what is this?`,
  opts:[`An appraisal cost`,
        `An internal failure cost`,
        `An external failure cost`,
        `A prevention cost`],
  a:3, why:`Prevention costs are spent to keep defects from happening in the first place, such as training, procedures and design reviews. Appraisal costs are the near miss. They're also a cost of conformance, but they pay for checking work, such as inspections and tests, not for preventing defects. Internal and external failure costs come after a defect exists, found before or after delivery.`,
  task:`R7`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`An agile team's stories all pass their acceptance tests, but users say the new app is too slow at busy times. Nothing in the team's quality standards mentions speed. What should the team do?`,
  opts:[`Write a single story to make the whole app faster`,
        `Ask users to avoid the app at peak times`,
        `Add performance standards to the definition of done`,
        `Test the app's performance once before the final release`],
  a:2, why:`Non-functional needs like speed apply to every increment, so they belong in the definition of done, with a measurable standard that every story is checked against. A single story to fix speed is the near miss. It may fix today's problem, but without a standard each new story can make the app slow again. Testing once before release finds problems too late, and asking users to work around the app isn't quality.`,
  task:`R7`, domain:`Process`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`Inspectors on a hybrid project need a quick way to tally the types of defects they find during inspections, so the team can see which types are most common. Which tool fits?`,
  opts:[`A fishbone diagram`,
        `A check sheet`,
        `A control chart`,
        `A flowchart`],
  a:1, why:`A check sheet is a simple form for tallying defects by type as they're found, and its data can feed a Pareto chart later. A fishbone diagram is the near miss. It's a quality tool too, but it explores possible causes of a problem instead of counting what's found. A control chart tracks a process over time, and a flowchart maps the steps of a process.`,
  task:`R7`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`A hybrid project's quality plan says the new customer portal must be "fast and reliable." Testers say they can't tell when they've met that. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Define measurable metrics for speed and reliability`,
        `Let the testers judge it based on their own experience`,
        `Ask users to rate the portal after launch`,
        `Remove the requirement from the quality plan`],
  a:0, why:`Quality metrics turn a goal into something that can be measured, such as page load time under a stated load, or uptime percent. Without them, nobody can tell whether the work passes. Asking users to rate it after launch is the near miss. User feedback is useful, but it comes too late to guide testing. Judgment varies from tester to tester, and removing the requirement drops a real need.`,
  task:`R7`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`Testing an airbag inflator destroys it. The team needs to know whether a batch of 10,000 meets the standard. What should the quality plan use?`,
  opts:[`Testing of every inflator in the batch`,
        `A visual inspection of every inflator`,
        `Statistical sampling of the batch`,
        `The supplier's quality certificate alone`],
  a:2, why:`When testing destroys the item, or testing everything costs too much, statistical sampling tests a planned number of items and uses the results to judge the batch. Relying on the supplier's certificate is the near miss. It's useful evidence, but on its own it isn't the project's own check of a safety part. Testing every item would leave nothing to ship, and a visual look can't find what the test checks.`,
  task:`R7`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`The client's quality requirements for a new office building include a minimum share of recycled content in its concrete. A supplier offers a cheaper mix that meets every strength test but has no recycled content. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Accept the mix that passes every strength test`,
        `Hold to the requirement unless the client changes it`,
        `Use the cheaper mix and report the shortfall at handover`,
        `Ask the supplier to certify the mix as recycled`],
  a:1, why:`Sustainability requirements written into the client's quality criteria are part of quality, just like strength. The cheaper mix doesn't meet them, so it's out unless the client changes the requirement through change control. Accepting it because it's strong is the near miss. Passing strength tests is necessary, but quality means meeting all the requirements, not just some. Reporting the shortfall later hides a known gap, and a false certificate is fraud.`,
  task:`R7`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`In the utility app case, what should the project manager do about the release plan for the rule's start date?`,
  opts:[`Commit to all 210 points by the date and plan overtime`,
        `Ask the team to raise its velocity to 30 points`,
        `Ask the state for more time to meet the new rule`,
        `Put the rule's stories first and forecast 175 points`],
  a:3, why:`Seven sprints at the team's average of 25 points gives about 175 points. All 210 won't fit, but the 60 points the rule needs will, if they come first. The product owner moves the rule's stories to the top, and the project manager shares the forecast range with stakeholders. Asking the state for more time is the near miss. It's an option if the rule work itself couldn't fit, but here it does. Committing all 210 with overtime and demanding a higher velocity both ignore the team's measured pace.`,
  task:`R8`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid`, case:`m2c1`, cq:1 },

{ q:`The chart shows a team's velocity for its first eight sprints. The release has 150 points left. Which forecast should the project manager give stakeholders?`,
  fig:`<prop type="chart" title="Velocity, points per sprint, sprints 1 to 8" labels="S1,S2,S3,S4,S5,S6,S7,S8" values="12,30,19,27,25,24,26,25"></prop>`,
  opts:[`About five sprints, based on the best sprint`,
        `Exactly six sprints, as a firm commitment`,
        `About 13 sprints, based on the first sprint`,
        `Six to seven sprints, based on the last five`],
  a:3, why:`Early sprints are often unsteady while a team settles, and this team has held steady at 24 to 27 points for the last five sprints. Their average, about 25, gives six sprints, and the slowest of them gives seven, so six to seven is the honest range. A firm six is the near miss. Six is the likeliest result, but a single committed number hides the real spread. Using the best sprint or the first sprint picks an outlier.`,
  task:`R8`, domain:`Process`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`Using the network diagram, activity C will start 4 days late. What happens to the project's end date?`,
  fig:`<prop type="chart" kind="network" title="Network diagram, durations in days" nodes="A:4,B:6,C:3,D:5,E:2,F:4" edges="A>B,A>C,B>D,C>E,D>F,E>F"></prop>`,
  opts:[`The end date moves 4 days later`,
        `The end date doesn't change`,
        `The end date moves 2 days later`,
        `The critical path shifts to run through C`],
  a:1, why:`The critical path is A, B, D, F at 4 plus 6 plus 5 plus 4, or 19 days. The other path, A, C, E, F, takes 13 days, so C has 6 days of total float. A 4 day delay uses 4 of them, and the end date holds. Moving 4 days is the near miss. That would be true if C were on the critical path, but it isn't. Moving 2 days comes from miscounting the float, and the A, C, E, F path would still be 2 days shorter than the critical path. C now has only 2 days of float left, so it's worth watching.`,
  task:`R8`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`The schedule shows equipment ordering waiting for the design review to finish. The team admits this is how they like to work, not a requirement, and the project needs to recover two weeks. What kind of dependency is it, and what does that allow?`,
  opts:[`Mandatory, meaning the order has to wait for the review`,
        `External, meaning the vendor controls the timing`,
        `Discretionary, meaning ordering could start earlier`,
        `Internal, meaning only the sponsor can change it`],
  a:2, why:`A discretionary dependency is preferred logic, chosen from best practice or habit, not required by the nature of the work. It's the first place to look when fast tracking, accepting the added risk that the design changes after ordering. Mandatory is the near miss. Some ordering does depend on design, but the team has said this one is a preference. External dependencies involve outside parties, and internal describes who controls a dependency, not whether it's required.`,
  task:`R8`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`A new agile team must size 200 backlog items before release planning next week. Planning poker on each item would take days. What should the team use?`,
  opts:[`Hours estimates for every item from the lead developer`,
        `Planning poker on only the first 20 items`,
        `Affinity estimating to group items by relative size`,
        `The same size for every item until work starts`],
  a:2, why:`Affinity estimating lets a team sort many items quickly by relative size, often into buckets such as T-shirt sizes or point ranges, and that's enough for release planning. Planning poker on the first 20 is the near miss. It's accurate for those items, but it leaves 180 unsized, and release planning needs the whole picture. One person's hours estimate isn't a team estimate, and giving everything the same size tells nobody anything.`,
  task:`R8`, domain:`Process`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`An agile team's forecast shows the release landing three sprints after the date marketing wants. The sponsor offers to add four developers to the six person team next sprint. What should the project manager tell the sponsor?`,
  opts:[`New people will slow the team before they speed it up`,
        `Velocity will rise in step with the size of the team`,
        `The date will hold if the four start next sprint`,
        `The team must re-estimate its backlog for the new size`],
  a:0, why:`Adding people late rarely brings a date in. New members need time to learn the product, and the team spends its own time helping them, so velocity usually drops for a few sprints before it rises, and a bigger team has more to coordinate. The project manager explains this and works with the product owner on what can fit by the date. Re-estimating is the near miss. The team will need to watch its new velocity, but story points measure relative size, so the backlog doesn't need resizing. Expecting velocity to rise in step, or the date to hold, overstates what four new people can do in three sprints.`,
  task:`R8`, domain:`Process`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`A project's schedule is limited by a few scarce specialists. The project manager removes the safety margin from each activity estimate and places one shared buffer at the end of the longest resource-constrained path. What technique is this?`,
  opts:[`Critical path method`,
        `Resource smoothing`,
        `Rolling wave planning`,
        `Critical chain method`],
  a:3, why:`The critical chain method accounts for limited resources and pools individual safety margins into buffers, such as a project buffer at the end, which are watched as the project runs. The critical path method is the near miss. It finds the longest path and the float, but it doesn't strip padding from activities into shared buffers. Resource smoothing adjusts resource use within float, and rolling wave planning adds detail over time.`,
  task:`R8`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`A hybrid project's software release depends on a vendor delivering hardware on May 1. The vendor's date appears nowhere in the project schedule. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Add the delivery as an external dependency and track it`,
        `Ask the vendor to deliver earlier than May 1`,
        `Let the agile team plan around the hardware once it arrives`,
        `Add a note about the vendor to the risk register only`],
  a:0, why:`Work the project depends on from outside parties belongs in the schedule as an external dependency or milestone, linked to the work it drives, so its effect on the dates can be seen and tracked. A risk register note is the near miss. The vendor's date may also be a risk, but without it in the schedule nobody can see what slips if it's late. Asking for an earlier date doesn't fix the missing link, and planning around it on arrival gives no warning.`,
  task:`R8`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`At the end of a sprint, a team reports that 10 of its 12 stories are "almost done." None of them meet the definition of done. How should the sprint's progress be reported?`,
  opts:[`As zero stories done this sprint`,
        `As about 80 percent of the work done`,
        `As 10 of the 12 stories done`,
        `As on track, pending final testing`],
  a:0, why:`Agile teams count only work that meets the definition of done. Partly finished stories earn no credit, which keeps progress honest and brings problems out early. About 80 percent is the near miss. It reflects real effort, but it estimates partial work, which is exactly what the definition of done is meant to avoid. Counting 10 as done is false, and "on track" hides that nothing is finished.`,
  task:`R9`, domain:`Process`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`A predictive project was due to finish two months ago and still has some work left. Its SPI has crept up from 0.80 to 0.98. The sponsor says the schedule must be nearly back on track. What should the project manager explain?`,
  opts:[`The project has made up most of its delay`,
        `The baseline must have been moved to the right`,
        `The team has been ahead of plan for two months`,
        `SPI heads toward 1.0 as a late project ends`],
  a:3, why:`SPI compares earned value with planned value, and once the planned finish date passes, planned value is fixed at the full budget. Every bit of work done after that raises earned value toward the same total, so SPI climbs toward 1.0 even while the project stays late. The project manager should report the forecast finish date, not lean on the index. A moved baseline is the near miss. Rebaselining can lift SPI too, but nothing here says the baseline changed, and the late finish explains the rise on its own. Neither a recovered delay nor two months ahead of plan fits a project that's still unfinished after its due date.`,
  task:`R9`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`Before a status meeting, the project manager sees that the finance system's actual cost for the project is $60,000 higher than the project's own cost report. What should the project manager do first?`,
  opts:[`Find out why the two figures differ before reporting`,
        `Report the finance system's figure as the true one`,
        `Report the project's own figure as it stands`,
        `Report both figures and let the meeting decide`],
  a:0, why:`Status has to be accurate, and two sources that disagree by $60,000 mean one is wrong or they count different things, such as accruals or overhead. Reconciling them first is what makes the report trustworthy. Reporting both figures is the near miss. It's transparent, but it hands the meeting a puzzle the project manager should solve. Picking either figure without knowing why they differ may report the wrong number.`,
  task:`R9`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`An AI tool forecasts a release date from the team's past velocity. The team points out that the next six weeks include holidays when half of them will be away. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Use the AI tool's forecast as it is`,
        `Ask the AI tool to run the forecast again`,
        `Stop forecasting until after the holidays`,
        `Adjust the forecast for the holidays`],
  a:3, why:`An AI forecast is only as good as the data behind it, and this one can't know about the holidays. The team's knowledge should adjust the forecast, with the adjustment explained so stakeholders see why it differs from the tool's. Running the tool again is the near miss. It may give a slightly different number, but from the same data, still missing the holidays. Using the forecast as is ignores known facts, and dropping forecasting leaves stakeholders guessing.`,
  task:`R9`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`Customers complain that their requests take too long. The team measures from when work starts to when it's done, and that number looks fine. Which measure reflects what customers experience?`,
  opts:[`Cycle time, from start of work to done`,
        `Velocity, in points per sprint`,
        `Lead time, from request to delivery`,
        `Throughput, in items per week`],
  a:2, why:`Lead time runs from when the customer asks to when they get it, including time waiting in the backlog, so it's what customers feel. Cycle time is the near miss. It's what the team has been measuring, from start of work to done, and it leaves out the waiting. Velocity and throughput measure how much the team completes, not how long one request takes.`,
  task:`R9`, domain:`Process`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`Team leads send the project manager raw data each week: actual start and finish dates and hours worked. The project manager compares it with the plan and writes up the variances for the sponsor. What is the raw data called?`,
  opts:[`Work performance information`,
        `Work performance reports`,
        `Work performance data`,
        `Change requests`],
  a:2, why:`Work performance data is the raw observation from the work, such as dates, hours and counts. Comparing it with the plan turns it into work performance information, and the write-up for the sponsor is a work performance report. Work performance information is the near miss. It's the next step, after the data has been analyzed against the plan. Change requests may come out of the analysis, but they aren't the data.`,
  task:`R9`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`A work package has a budget of $40,000. It's 60 percent complete, and $30,000 has been spent on it. What are its earned value and cost variance?`,
  opts:[`EV of $24,000 and CV of plus $6,000`,
        `EV of $30,000 and CV of plus $10,000`,
        `EV of $24,000 and CV of minus $6,000`,
        `EV of $18,000 and CV of minus $12,000`],
  a:2, why:`Earned value is percent complete times the budget: 60 percent of $40,000 is $24,000. Cost variance is EV minus AC: $24,000 minus $30,000 is minus $6,000, so the work cost more than its budgeted value. Plus $6,000 is the near miss. The size is right, but it subtracts EV from AC, which flips the sign. EV of $30,000 uses actual cost, and $18,000 takes 60 percent of the wrong figure.`,
  task:`R9`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`At closeout, the project manager is archiving project records. A team member asks how long the records must be kept. What decides this?`,
  opts:[`The project manager's sense of what's useful`,
        `The sponsor's preference at the closeout meeting`,
        `The team's vote at the final retrospective`,
        `The organization's records retention policy`],
  a:3, why:`How long records are kept is set by organizational policy, often driven by legal, contract or regulatory requirements, and the project manager follows it when archiving. The sponsor's preference is the near miss. The sponsor has authority on many project matters, but retention rules come from the organization and the law, not from one person's preference. The project manager's own judgment and a team vote don't override policy either.`,
  task:`R10`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`A hybrid project's last deliverable has been accepted. Two purchase orders are still open, and a test environment the project rented is still billing monthly. What should the project manager do before closing?`,
  opts:[`Close the open orders and shut down the unused environment`,
        `Leave them for the finance team to sort out after closure`,
        `Keep the environment running in case the product needs it`,
        `Note them in the final report and then close the project`],
  a:0, why:`Closing a project includes closing its financials. Open commitments are closed or settled, and resources that still cost money are released, with operations agreeing to anything they'll keep. Noting them in the final report is the near miss. It's honest, but the orders stay open and the environment keeps billing. Leaving them to finance shifts the project's work onto others, and keeping the environment just in case spends money nobody approved.`,
  task:`R10`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`An agile project's funded scope is done and the project will close, but the product will keep evolving under a product team. The backlog still has 80 items. What should happen to it?`,
  opts:[`Delete the remaining items at project close`,
        `Keep the project open until the backlog is empty`,
        `Hand the backlog to the product team's owner`,
        `Archive the backlog with the project records`],
  a:2, why:`Projects end, and products carry on. The remaining backlog passes to whoever owns the product from here, so its value isn't lost. Archiving it with the project records is the near miss. The project's records do get archived, but a live backlog filed away stops being used. Deleting it throws away known needs, and keeping the project open until the backlog is empty means it never closes.`,
  task:`R10`, domain:`Process`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`A seller has delivered everything under a fixed price contract, and the buyer has accepted it. What should the buyer's project manager make sure happens to close the contract?`,
  opts:[`Pay the final invoice and file the contract away`,
        `Hold a lessons learned session with the seller`,
        `Confirm every term is met and give written notice`,
        `Ask the seller to sign a closeout memo for the buyer`],
  a:2, why:`Closing a procurement means confirming all terms and deliverables are complete, settling any claims, making final payment, giving the seller formal written notice that the contract is complete, and filing the records, including a seller performance review. Paying the final invoice is the near miss. Payment is part of it, but paying and filing skips formal notice and the check that every term was met. A lessons learned session is useful but doesn't close the contract, and the buyer gives the notice, not the seller.`,
  task:`R10`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`A building is ready to hand over except for a few minor finishing items. The client is willing to accept it now. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Get acceptance with a dated list of the open items`,
        `Hold the handover until every item is finished`,
        `Take the client's word and close the project`,
        `Close and fix items if the client calls`],
  a:0, why:`Formal acceptance can be given with known minor items, as long as they're listed with owners and dates in the sign-off. That protects both sides and lets the client use the building. Holding the handover is the near miss. It's tidy, but it delays the client's use for items they've agreed can wait. Taking the client's word without a written record, or closing and waiting for calls, leaves the open items undefined.`,
  task:`R10`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`A hybrid project's final software release goes live next week. The support desk has seen the software only once, in a demo. What should the project manager plan?`,
  opts:[`A support manual sent to the desk on launch day`,
        `Asking the desk to send tickets to the vendor`,
        `A short period where the team backs up the support desk`,
        `Delaying the release until the support desk is fully trained`],
  a:2, why:`A transition period where project team members work beside the support desk after go live, often called hypercare, catches early problems and passes on knowledge while it's fresh. Delaying the release is the near miss. More training helps, but holding the release delays value when a support period can cover the gap. A manual on launch day comes too late to absorb, and sending tickets to the vendor bypasses the team that knows the system.`,
  task:`R10`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid` },

]});
